#speculation(title: [GPCR Autoantibody-Mediated Mast Cell Sensitization — Bidirectional Amplification Loop])[
*Certainty: 0.35.* GPCR autoantibodies (β2-adrenergic, M3 muscarinic) may directly sensitize mast cells, which express the cognate receptors. β2-AR autoantibodies can chronically activate mast cell β2-AR, leading to desensitization and removal of the adrenergic brake on degranulation. M3 autoantibodies directly activate muscarinic receptors on mast cells, triggering histamine and tryptase release. This creates a bidirectional amplification loop: GPCR autoantibodies → mast cell degranulation → inflammatory mediators → increased BBB permeability → more autoantibody CNS access → brainstem neuroinflammation → autonomic dysregulation → further immune dysregulation. The loop explains why both MCAS-targeted treatment and immunomodulation show benefit — they interrupt different points of the same autoantibody-mast cell loop. Because these autoantibodies are IgG, a parallel and non-exclusive route is possible: circulating IgG immune complexes can cross-link mast cell FcγRI/FcγRIIA and trigger degranulation without any direct receptor agonism @Okayama2000FcgammaRIHumanMast @Woolhiser2001IgG1MastFcgammaRI. @Blitshteyn2026Neuroimmune @Blitshteyn2025Neuroinflammation

This IgG arm is conditional rather than constitutive. FcγRI is only weakly expressed on resting human mast cells and is markedly up-regulated by IFN-γ @Okayama2000FcgammaRIHumanMast, so the route would be open mainly during an interferon-active phase. That phase is an explicit gating condition rather than an established ME/CFS state: whether any ME/CFS phase supplies the relevant IFN-γ signal is analogical and unmeasured, because no mast-cell FcγRI measurement exists in ME/CFS. The arm is also a separate lever from direct GPCR agonism, since it depends on immune-complex cross-linking rather than receptor binding, so the two routes may operate together, alone, or not at all. Confidence in this arm is low, resting on in-vitro cultured-mast-cell data alone.

Among IgG subclasses, only IgG1 drives degranulation in the FcγRI-dependent system studied (CD34⁺-derived human mast cells); IgG2, IgG3, and IgG4 produce minimal release there @Woolhiser2001IgG1MastFcgammaRI. The subclass profile specific to the skin FcγRIIA route is not established by these data. The subclass of a GPCR autoantibody therefore decides, not merely its presence, whether it also provokes mast cells through FcγR: an IgG1 anti-GPCR antibody would carry a dual route (direct receptor agonism plus Fc-dependent cross-linking), whereas an IgG4 anti-GPCR antibody would not. This attaches a mast-cell consequence to the paper's existing IgG-subclass discussion in the hypothesis registry @Hartwig2020.

*Replication status:* Mast cell β2-AR expression and M3-muscarinic expression are established in the mast cell biology literature. The specific GPCR autoantibody-mast cell interaction has not been tested in ME/CFS. This is a mechanistic synthesis from two independently established lines.

*Limitations:* No direct evidence of GPCR autoantibody binding to mast cells from ME/CFS patients. The direction of β2-AR autoantibody effect (agonistic vs antagonistic) on mast cells is unknown. The loop model is a hypothesis requiring direct cellular testing.

*Falsifiable prediction:* Plasma tryptase will correlate with β2-AR AAb titer (r \> 0.4, p \< 0.01) in ME/CFS patients (n ≥ 30). Falsified if r ≤ 0.2 or no significant correlation.

*Consequence:* If confirmed, one autoantibody measurement would mark both a driver and a possible treatment point, and mast-cell-directed and immunomodulatory treatments would act at different links of the same loop; while unmeasured, the loop explains overlapping treatment effects only as a hypothesis and cannot direct their use.
] <spec:gpcr-mast-cell-loop>
